Below is a minimal “recipe” you can drop into your test‑suite or helper class to fire off the **master‑node** side of the CCR usage collection routine and wire up a simple `ActionListener`.  
Everything is expressed in plain Java 8 style so you can copy/paste directly into an existing project that already has access to the Elasticsearch internals (`org.elasticsearch.xpack.ccr.action.CCRUsageTransportAction`).  

```java
import org.elasticsearch.cluster.ClusterState;
import org.elasticsearch.common.util.concurrent.EsExecutors;
import org.elasticsearch.tasks.TaskId;
import org.elasticsearch.transport.TransportService;
import org.elasticsearch.threadpool.ThreadPool;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Build/obtain the pieces you’ll hand over to the transport action.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
TaskId   task          = new TaskId("my-task", System.currentTimeMillis(), ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong());
XPackUsageRequest usageReq    = new XPackUsageRequest();           // ← whatever fields you care about
ClusterState clusterState     = ClusterState.builder(ClusterName.DEFAULT).build();
ThreadPool threadPool         = /* obtain from your node / test harness */;
Executor executor             = EsExecutors.newDirectExecutor();

```

> *If you’re writing unit tests* – you probably already have mocks/fakes for `ClusterState` and `XPackUsageRequest`; just feed them straight through.

---

### 2️⃣  Wire the listener that receives the result

```java
// A very small demo listener that simply prints out the raw JSON payload
ActionListener<BytesReference> listener = new ActionListener<>() {
    @Override public void onResponse(BytesReference bytesRef) {
        try {
            String json = BytesReference.toUtf8String(bytesRef);
            System.out.println("[CCR Usage] Received reply:\n" + json);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    @Override public void onFailure(Exception exception) {
        System.err.println("[CCR Usage] Failed to fetch data:");
        exception.printStackTrace();
    }
};
```

> In production you would normally deserialize the byte array back into a POJO (`CCRUsageStats`) and push it onto some monitoring pipeline.

---

### 3️⃣  Call the transport action’s master‑side handler

Now we actually trigger the logic that runs on the master node.  
Because the real implementation lives inside Elasticsearch itself, here we illustrate the *call pattern* rather than re‑implementing all internal plumbing.

```java
/**
 * Execute the master operation that collects CCR usage metrics.
 *
 * @param task      Identifier used internally by TransportService to track progress.
 * @param request   The original XPackUsageRequest coming from the client.
 * @param state     Current snapshot of the cluster state at the time of execution.
 * @param listener  Callback invoked once the calculation finishes (or fails).
 */
public static void runCcrMasterOp(
        TaskId task,
        XPackUsageRequest request,
        ClusterState state,
        ActionListener<BytesReference> listener)
{
    // Instantiate the transport action (normally done during node startup)
    CCRUsageTransportAction ccrUsageAction =
            new CCRUsageTransportAction(threadPool);

    // Fire the master‑only part of the action:
    // - It will compute #followerIndices & #autoFollowPatterns
    // - Serialize those counts into a JSON blob
    // - Deliver the blob to our supplied listener
    //
    // Internally this calls something like:
    //   ccrUsageAction.masterOperation(task, request, state, listener);
    //
    // For illustration purposes we expose the same API surface:
    ccrUsageAction.masterOperation(task, request, state, listener);
}
```

Call it like this:

```java
runCcrMasterOp(task, usageReq, clusterState, listener);
```

When executed against a live cluster, the above will:

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| `masterOperation(...)` | Runs only on the master node. |
| Counts followers | Uses `FollowerIndexMetadata.getAll(state)` to count active follower indices. |
| Counts auto‑follow patterns | Looks at `AutoFollowPatternMetaData` entries in the cluster metadata. |
| Serialises → JSON | Builds a lightweight map such as `{ "follower_indices": 12, "auto_follow_patterns": 5 }`. |
| Sends back | Wraps the map into a `BytesArray` and hands it to the supplied `ActionListener`. |

That’s essentially everything you need to get started gathering CCR usage stats from the master node. If you’d prefer to see the exact source code path, look under `x-pack/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/ccr/action/CCRUsageTransportAction.java` where the `masterOperation()` method does the heavy lifting.
